Header Only Version
==================================================================
Just copy the files to your build tree and use a C++11 compiler.  
Or use CMake:
``` 
  add_executable(example_header_only example.cpp)
  target_link_libraries(example_header_only spdlog::spdlog_header_only)
```

Compiled Library Version
==================================================================
CMake:
```  
  add_executable(example example.cpp)
  target_link_libraries(example spdlog::spdlog)
```

Or copy files src/*.cpp to your build tree and pass the -DSPDLOG_COMPILED_LIB to the compiler.

C++20 Module Version (experimental)
==================================================================
Build spdlog with -DSPDLOG_BUILD_MODULE=ON, then:
```
  find_package(spdlog CONFIG REQUIRED COMPONENTS module)
  add_executable(example_module module_example.cpp)
  target_link_libraries(example_module spdlog::spdlog_module)
  set_target_properties(example_module PROPERTIES CXX_SCAN_FOR_MODULES ON)
```

Requirements: CMake 3.28 or newer, the Ninja (1.11+) or Visual Studio 17.4+ generator, and
gcc 15+, clang 17+ or MSVC 19.34+. Configuring with SPDLOG_BUILD_MODULE=ON on anything older
fails with an explanation rather than a confusing build error.

This is a *native* module: spdlog's headers are annotated with SPDLOG_EXPORT (see
include/spdlog/namespace.h) and compiled directly inside the module's purview
(src/spdlog.cppm), so every exported declaration is attached to (owned by) the named
module `spdlog`, exactly as if you had written the library as a module from the start.
Things worth knowing, all consequences of that design choice:

* Do NOT mix `import spdlog;` and `#include <spdlog/...>` in the same program, not even
  across different translation units. Module-attached entities get module ownership -
  different name mangling on MSVC, strong ownership on Clang - so the two styles produce
  two distinct sets of entities (two different spdlog::logger types, two different
  registries, ...) even though the names are identical. Linking both together is an ODR
  violation and a source of very confusing link and runtime errors (e.g. spdlog::get() in
  one TU will never find a logger registered from the other). Pick one style per program.
  (A companion PR implements an alternative *wrapper*-style module, built specifically to
  support mixing the two - see that PR if you need it.)
* The module cannot link a separately-built spdlog::spdlog (static or shared) library, for
  the same reason. It is built in header-only mode instead: every spdlog definition -
  normally emitted once into libspdlog from src/*.cpp - is instead emitted once into the
  module's own object code, and SPDLOG_COMPILED_LIB is never defined for this target.
  There is therefore no such thing as a "header-only module" vs. "compiled module"
  distinction; spdlog::spdlog_module is the only module target, and it is self-contained.
* Binary module interfaces are not distributable. What gets installed is the module
  source, <prefix>/share/spdlog/module/spdlog.cppm, and it is recompiled inside your
  build. Your compiler therefore does not have to match the one that built libspdlog, but
  your C++ standard and your SPDLOG_* configuration do - exactly as with the headers.
* `import spdlog;` gives you spdlog's names only. Standard library names (std::shared_ptr,
  std::string, ...) still come from the usual #includes, and a module cannot export
  macros, so the SPDLOG_INFO(..) family lives in <spdlog/macros.h>.
* On MSVC, include your standard headers *before* `import spdlog;`. Including one
  afterwards produces a cascade of C2572 "redefinition of default argument" errors inside
  the MSVC standard library. Clang accepts either order.
* fmt stays attached to the global module, not to spdlog. The fmt names needed to
  specialize fmt::formatter<T> are re-exported by the module; under SPDLOG_USE_STD_FORMAT,
  #include <format> yourself to specialize std::formatter<T>. Do not #include a header that
  declares spdlog's own entities (e.g. <spdlog/logger.h>) alongside the import - see above.
* Only sinks with no external dependency are exported: qt_sinks, mongo_sink, kafka_sink,
  systemd_sink, syslog_sink, tcp_sink, udp_sink, android_sink and loki_sink stay
  header-only and can be #included next to `import spdlog;`.
* No pkg-config file is generated for the module; modules cannot be expressed in
  pkg-config.
* If you override SPDLOG_NAMESPACE or SPDLOG_NAMESPACE_BEGIN/END, pass the same flags to
  the build that compiles spdlog.cppm.

Important Information for Compilation:
==================================================================
* If you encounter compilation errors with gcc 4.8.x, please note that gcc 4.8.x does not fully support C++11. In such cases, consider upgrading your compiler or using a different version that fully supports C++11 standards

Tested on:  
gcc 4.8.1 and above
clang 3.5
Visual Studio 2013